# DataFlowBench JavaScript taint-modeling declarations, in the pinned Joern
# distribution's own textual flow-semantics format
# (io.joern.dataflowengineoss.semanticsloader.FullNameSemanticsParser).
#
# What is declared here is docs/modeling-matrix.md's analyzer-neutral model,
# unchanged; how it is declared is Joern's own surface. Positions follow
# Joern's convention: 0 is the receiver, 1 is the first declared parameter,
# and -1 is the return value, so the declaration language's position 0 is
# index 1 here. A method named with no mappings is NilSemantics -- taint does
# not leave it -- which is the surface for the `sanitizer` role.
#
# The two surface facts recorded beside the Python semantics hold here too and
# are repeated because each fails silently: a blank line anywhere in this file
# drops every declaration, and `#` opens a comment while `//` does not. There
# are therefore no blank lines below, only `#` comment lines.
#
# Method identity is the CPG's `methodFullName`, which the pinned `jssrc2cpg`
# frontend spells `<file>::program:<member>` for a top-level function and
# `<file>::program:<Type>:<member>` for a class member. That is the JavaScript
# spelling of the declaration language's type-plus-member identity.
#
# A JAVASCRIPT-SPECIFIC SURFACE FACT, MEASURED AGAINST THE PINNED 4.0.610
# FRONTEND RATHER THAN ASSUMED (docs/javascript-modeling.md records the probe):
# a *call* to a class method -- static or instance -- carries
# `methodFullName = <unknownFullName>`, and a call to an object-literal member
# carries the member's inferred *structural type*
# (`{ scrub: (value: ANY) => ANY; ... }:scrub`), which is a property of the
# object's shape rather than of the entity. Only a top-level function call
# carries the entity's own full name. The declared sanitizer is therefore a
# top-level function in the fixtures, which is the one JavaScript shape whose
# identity the frontend exposes; the persistence pair below is declared at the
# identity the model names, and whether the frontend binds a call to it is the
# measurement, not the artifact's business.
#
# Category S -- declared sources and sinks -- needs no entry here: Joern's
# source and sink identities are the query's own selectors, which the runner
# binds to the declared identity read off the fixture's DFB-SOURCE and
# DFB-SINK marker lines. Category E likewise: an entry-point root is a
# parameter node, not a flow mapping.
#
# Categories P (templates 3, 4) and O (templates 7, 8) need no entry here
# either, and deliberately have none: Amendment A2 moved Joern's propagator and
# summary cells to unsupported activation, because `FlowSemantic` mappings on
# the pinned 4.0.610 are additive over the engine's default unmodeled-call
# pass-through and cannot restrict it. A declaration for `Opaque.carry`,
# `Opaque.select`, `Bridge.pass`, or `Bridge.deposit` would not be
# load-bearing, and this matrix's equivalence contract forbids an artifact from
# declaring a category its partition marks unsupported. The measurements that
# established this are retained in docs/javascript-modeling.md.
# --- No-flow declarations (NilSemantics) ---
# Templates 5 and 6: `sanitizer`, in: 0, on the declared `scrub`. `scrub`'s
# body is the identity function, so this declaration -- not the body -- is what
# suppresses the flow. The sanitizer-shaped sibling `sanitize` is deliberately
# undeclared, so template 6's positive must still be reported.
"Clean.js::program:scrub"
# --- Flow mappings ---
# Templates 11 and 12: `store-write` (in: 1, key: 0) and `store-read`
# (out: return, key: 0) on `Store.put` and `Store.get`, sharing one store
# identity. The model declares the boundary: `put` maps its value parameter
# into its store receiver, and `get` maps its receiver to its return. Key and
# instance discrimination are left to the engine, which is the division the
# preregistration fixes.
"Store.js::program:Store:put" 2->0
"Store.js::program:Store:get" 0->-1
